==Old English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*obar}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*uber}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*upér}}, a comparative form of ''*upo''.

''Germanic cognates:'' {{cog|osx|oƀar}}, {{cog|nl|over}}, {{cog|goh|ubar}} (whence {{cog|de|über}}), {{cog|non|yfir}} (whence {{cog|sv|över}}), {{cog|got|𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂}}.

''Indo-European cognates:'' {{cog|grc|ὑπέρ}}, {{cog|la|super}}, {{cog|sga|for}} ({{cog|cy|gor-}}).

